  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1049 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nAnother rinse and repeat night is on display across the Mid-South\nwith current temperatures spanning the 50s and lower 60s under\nclear skies with light winds at the surface. We'll wake up to\nanother chilly morning with lows in the 40s to lower 50s and\nplenty of sunshine. Outside of some high cirrus Saturday will be\nmuch the same as Friday with highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s\nas upper-level ridging continues to dominate the weather pattern\nacross the region. As a very dry airmass remains parked over the\nMississippi Valley, dewpoints will fall into the 40s leading to \ncontinued fire weather concerns into the weekend. We ask you to \nplease continue to adhere to local burn bans, properly dispose of \ncigarettes and matches, and avoid using equipment that creates \nsparks.\n\nMoving through the weekend and into early next week, little to no\nchange is of note as aforementioned upper-level ridging continues\nto build over the Mid-South. Afternoon highs will remain\ngenerally 5 to 10 degrees above normal with a slight cooldown\nearly next week as highs begin to span the upper 70s. Cool\nmornings remain the trend with lows dropping into the 40s and\nlower 50s each day. Ensembles are in decent agreement with upper-\nlevel ridging finally beginning to break down come mid-week as\ndual upper-level low pressure systems (one from the northern\nRockies and another from the Pacific Northwest) begin to push\ninto the upper Midwest. Though much of the moisture looks to push\nnorth of the Mid-South come Wednesday, an increase in dewpoints\nis anticipated helping to potentially alleviate fire weather\nconcerns.\n\nAs far as precipitation is concerned, our best shot looks to\noccur late with LREF hinting at a northwest flow regime beginning\nto build across the Mississippi Valley. How much moisture the Mid-\nSouth is able to retain will depend greatly on the strength of\nthe aforementioned low pressure systems. Some potential good\nnews, the latest CPC 6-10 day outlook has us favoring (40-50%)\nabove normal precipitation chances heading into early November.\nAny precipitation will be appreciated as many areas across the\nMid-South remain under Extreme Drought (D3) and a few areas under\nExceptional Drought (D4) conditions.\n\nAEH",
